comments.body:
Yes, 60 lines for a struct definition, without any other ordering, feels a bit much to have without any more structure. (TBF there are — good! —  comments that make a lot of that, but, still, it’s 60 lines that must be understood.)

In such situations, I’d expect some further structure, maybe just as a blank-line separated “paragraphs”; and those “paragraphs” can also serve to document more rules
```go
type netaarkNetwork {
   // Global paths on this OS: they never change after this structure is created.
   netavarkBinary string // …
   aardvarkBinary string // …

  // Settings relevant for this process: they never change after this structure is created.
  networkConfigDir string // …
  networkRunDir string // …
  networkRootless bool // …

  // Runtime state
  lock *lockfile.LockFile // better documentation than we have now for this
  // These fields can only be accessed with lock held
  …
}
```

or something _very vaguely_ like that (I didn’t read the package and I have no idea what I’m talking about).

---

Whatever happens here, please keep the field order in the declaration and in the constructor the same, to make it _a bit_ easier to notice missing field initialization.
